## Your rights at work

An eating disorder can count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life. Your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- **protected breaks** for meals and snacks
- time off for **treatment and appointments**
- a **phased return** after time off
- flexibility if you are attending a **day programme**

**Access to Work** offers a free **Mental Health Support Service**.

## Benefits

PIP is based on how your condition affects you on **most days**. An eating disorder can affect:

- **eating and drinking:** needing **prompting** or **supervision** to eat, which is part of the PIP activity on "taking nutrition"
- **preparing food:** needing supervision or support to prepare meals
- **mixing with other people** and **planning journeys:** if anxiety causes overwhelming distress
- **managing treatment:** needing support with an eating plan or medicine

Children and young people under 16 may qualify for **DLA** if they need much more care and supervision than other children their age, for example at mealtimes.

